\section{Comments}{

With \code{comments = TRUE}, comments are kept and attached to the R objects
they describe, using R's \code{\link[=comment]{comment()}} attribute:
\itemize{
\item Comments on their own line(s) before an object member or array element
belong to that member or element.
\item A comment that follows a value on the same line (before or after the
comma) belongs to that value.
\item Comments before or after the top-level value, and comments directly
inside an empty object/array or before its closing bracket, belong to
the enclosing object/array (or to the top-level value).
}

Comment delimiters are removed: \verb{// note} and \verb{/* note */} both become
\code{"note"}. When an element has several comments, \code{comment()} is a character
vector with one element per comment.

Some JSON5 values have no R object that can hold an attribute: \code{null}
(\code{NULL} in R), the elements of an array that jsonlite simplified to an
atomic vector or matrix, and the rows and cells of a data frame. Their
comments are stored on the closest enclosing R object, in the attribute
\code{"json5_comments"}. It is a named list that maps a relative
\href{https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc6901/}{JSON Pointer} (such as \code{"/2"} or
\code{"/0/name"}) to a character vector of comments. \code{\link[=to_json5]{to_json5()}} reads both
attributes, so comments survive a read/write round trip. Use
\code{simplifyVector = FALSE} to keep every element as its own R object.
}

\section{Numbers}{

Hexadecimal literals are converted exactly to decimal before they reach
jsonlite. With \code{bigint_as_char = TRUE}, integers that do not fit in a
